Julian how did you become an activist  and do you consider yourself to be an  activist today I became an activist  because someone asked me to join an  activist movement i was asked by a  fellow student in Atlanta at Morehouse  College to join the beginning of  Atlanta's participation in the sit-in  movement and he was so compelling I  couldn't say no to him and so I found  myself caught up in it and I've been  caught up with it ever since so yes I do  consider myself an activist today  perhaps a different kind of activism but  it's still activism for the same sorts  of causes although the causes have grown  but yes I am still an activist was that  an easy decision though I mean when you  say someone asked you to join the sit-in  we're talking about a sit-in movement in  Georgia in a period in which that was  not a safe thing to do well I knew the  possibility was that we'd get arrested I  knew getting arrested was likely to put  a mark on your forehead do not employ I  knew that the possibility was of some  harsh treatment at the hands of the  police or white bystanders but I also  knew that other people like myself young  people young black people and other  college campuses like mine had done this  and they had braved these possibilities  without turning back and I thought I  could do the same and I found something  someplace in me that made me do the same  and so even though there was some  hesitation I could overcome it when you  made those choices knowing who your  parents were and you could tell us who  the word I know who they are how did  they deal with the choices you were  making at that time as a young man my  parents were academics my father was the  had been the president of Lincoln  University in Pennsylvania and almost I  also been the president of Fort Valley  State College in Georgia and at the time  this occurred he was Dean of the School  of Education Atlanta University my  mother had been a classroom teacher and  later become a librarian librarians are  there people interest in education and  so the idea that I would risk my  education by getting arrested or by  dropping out of college was just awful  to them but at the same time they were  very supportive of the civil rights  movement and encouraged me to get  engaged in it and so torn between these  two  the things and followed my promise that  I would go back to school someday and  finish my degree which I did too they  acquiesced
 

